{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mr5iict1lh3","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"ls20 collision repair walls color four and nine run fifteen","summary":"Run-15 dev repair of the ls20 world model program. Diagnosed every banked ls20 divergence: of 173 transitions across all 10 ls20 suites, the collision-free stored program passed 110 per-transition and failed 63 — and ALL 63 failures were one mode: the player unit (colors 9,12) was translated by the fixed action vector when live it stayed put (blocked by a wall). Derived the block predicate empirically: a move is blocked iff any leading-edge target cell (a player cell plus the action vector, landing outside the unit footprint) is out of bounds OR holds a wall color. Testing wall-color sets gave WALLS=(4,9) accuracy 0.988 with ZERO false blocks (color 4 is the maze wall, separate color-9 objects also block; color 3 is free floor and never blocked). Fix: snapshot a static obstacle set in init_state and block the translate in step when obstructed; walls modelled not masked. Replaced hardcoded is_win=False with a real state predicate over goal-cell contact (win evidence thin: no banked transition captured a level boundary, so it returns False until goal cells are seeded, not a constant). UNKNOWN retained only for the auto-changing HUD bar (color 11) and non-player regions. Result: 8/10 suites 100 percent, union 171/173=0.988 (up from collision-free 110/173=0.636), beating the run-14 0.93 bar. The 2 residual failures are color-3 free-floor blocks with no static wall present (genuinely unmodelable dynamic block), kept UNKNOWN not overfit. Reseeded to KB as note note-mr5i0uv2sdg (supersedes note-mr5d6cvqife) via inline write_program_revision so the daemon source-clusters it; native /note/get round-trips (chunk_count 9, byte_length 8294), extracts byte-functional-identical, and compiles in the sandbox. All 303 ewm tests pass and --smoke is green.","category":null,"tags":[],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"202